# M5 on-target test fixture: unit 1 should report this MTU via
# S2_DEVICEQUERY after OpenDevice() -- see sana2test.c's "config" sub-test.
# truncate=20/rxqueue=2 (pre-launch review pass) are ADDITIVE: unit 1's
# own "config" sub-test only ever does S2_DEVICEQUERY, no read/write
# traffic, so these are inert there -- they're picked up on entirely
# separate, later, fresh reopens of this same unit by the new
# "faulttruncate"/"faultrxqueue" sub-tests instead. Deliberately reusing
# unit 1 rather than adding two brand-new units: this device's per-unit
# static state is sizeable enough that even UNOPENED extra units measurably
# shrink bundled AROS's already-tight free-memory headroom for LATER
# dynamic allocations elsewhere in the same session (confirmed: adding 2
# fresh units for exactly this purpose reliably broke the unrelated
# "scheduled" sub-test's own replay-buffer allocation on bundled AROS
# specifically, while real Kickstart 1.3 stayed unaffected -- the same
# class of AROS memory-pressure regression M9/M14 already hit and
# resolved, this time avoided outright by not growing SANA2LOOP_NUM_UNITS
# at all).
# bps=57600/mintu=16 (M15) are additive the same way: bps= only changes
# S2_DEVICEQUERY output (asserted by the extended "config" sub-test);
# mintu=16 sits safely below every write this unit's other sub-tests
# perform (the shortest is PAYLOAD's 19 bytes) so it's inert everywhere
# except the dedicated "mintu" sub-test's own deliberate 8-byte write.
# addr= (M16) is additive the same way again: the pinned station address
# only matters to the dedicated "addr" sub-test's own assertions -- none
# of unit 1's other sub-tests ever assert a station or delivered address,
# only payloads/types/lengths (echo delivery itself is address-unfiltered).
mtu=1400
truncate=20
rxqueue=2
bps=57600
mintu=16
addr=02:AA:BB:CC:DD:01
